.TH ATLAS-GDB 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-gdb \- Run a program under a debugger: stop it, look at registers and memory, step instruction by instruction.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
gdb  \-q  \-\-args ./hello open\-sesame
 |    |       '\-\-\- everything after \-\-args is the program and its arguments
 |    '\-\-\- quiet: no banner
 '\-\-\- the debugger

inside gdb:   break main    run    next / step    info registers    x/20wx $esp    bt    continue    quit
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Binary exploitation: find the offset that overwrites the return address.
.IP \(bu 2
Reverse engineering: watch what a check compares.
.IP \(bu 2
See what a crashed program was doing.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fBbreak FUNC / b *ADDR\fR
Set a breakpoint.
.br
Use it when: Stop where something interesting happens.
.TP
\fBrun [args] / r\fR
Start the program.
.br
Use it when: Begin.
.TP
\fBnext / step / nexti / stepi\fR
Step over / into a call; by source line or instruction.
.br
Use it when: Follow the logic.
.TP
\fBcontinue / c\fR
Resume.
.br
Use it when: Run to the next breakpoint.
.TP
\fBinfo registers / p $rip\fR
Show registers.
.br
Use it when: Where is the program, what are the values.
.TP
\fBx/20wx $esp / x/s ADDR / x/10i $rip\fR
Examine memory: words, string, instructions.
.br
Use it when: Look at the stack, strings or code.
.TP
\fBdisassemble main / set disassembly\-flavor intel\fR
Show code; Intel syntax.
.br
Use it when: Read assembly.
.TP
\fBbt\fR
Backtrace: the call stack.
.br
Use it when: After a crash.
.TP
\fBpattern create N / pattern offset X\fR
Cyclic pattern helpers (pwndbg/GEF).
.br
Use it when: Find an overflow offset.
.TP
\fB\-ex 'cmd' \-batch\fR
Run commands non\-interactively.
.br
Use it when: Scripts.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. Break at main and look at registers"
\fB\-batch \-ex\fR runs gdb commands without the interactive prompt.
.PP
.nf
$ gdb \-q \-batch \-ex 'break main' \-ex 'run' \-ex 'info registers rip' ./hello
.fi
.PP
.nf
(expected, not captured) Breakpoint 1, 0x... in main () followed by a line like rip 0x55555555513d showing the instruction pointer.
.fi
.PP
\fBrip\fR (64\-bit instruction pointer) is where the program stopped.
.SS "2. Disassemble main in Intel syntax"
The same view as \fBobjdump \-d\fR but inside the debugger.
.PP
.nf
$ gdb \-q \-batch \-ex 'set disassembly\-flavor intel' \-ex 'disassemble main' ./hello
.fi
.PP
.nf
(expected, not captured) Assembly of main with addresses, including a call to strcmp and a conditional jump.
.fi
.PP
Find the \fBcmp\fR/\fBtest\fR and the following jump: that is the decision.
.SS "3. Crash it and read the registers"
This is the Narnia method: send a long input and see which bytes reach the instruction pointer.
.PP
.nf
$ gdb \-q \-batch \-ex 'run $(python3 \-c "print(chr(65)*200)")' \-ex 'info registers rip' ./hello
.fi
.PP
.nf
(expected, not captured) Possibly no crash for this program; for a vulnerable one: Program received signal SIGSEGV and rip showing the overwritten value (such as 0x4141414141414141).
.fi
.PP
If \fBrip\fR holds your pattern, you control execution: compute the offset with a cyclic pattern.
.SS "4. Print a string from memory"
\fBx/s\fR examines memory as a string; use \fBx/10i\fR for instructions and \fBx/20wx\fR for words.
.PP
.nf
$ gdb \-q \-batch \-ex 'break main' \-ex 'run' \-ex 'x/s $rip' ./hello
.fi
.PP
.nf
(expected, not captured) A line starting with the address and the bytes at it interpreted as text or instructions.
.fi
.PP
Learn the three formats: \fBs\fR string, \fBi\fR instruction, \fBx\fR hex.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
Stack addresses in gdb differ from the real run (environment size and path): sweep or leak the real address.
.IP \(bu 2
gdb disables ASLR by default; the real program may randomise.
.IP \(bu 2
Set\-uid programs lose privileges under gdb.
.IP \(bu 2
Install pwndbg or GEF for a readable display.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Find which instruction of \fBhello\fR compares your argument.
.IP 2. 3
Change \fBargv[1]\fR in memory so the check passes.
.IP 3. 3
Use \fBx/20wx $rsp\fR to dump the stack at \fBmain\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
pwndbg / GEF, objdump, ltrace / strace, ghidra, pwntools.
